A detector never sees token ids, only text, so it must re-tokenize. Decoding the 200 watermarked tokens and encoding the text again gives 199 tokens: the model had sampled " as" followed by "pect", and the tokenizer's own encoding of that text is the single token " aspect". That replaces three scored pairs with two, the pair (" begins", " with") drops out because the prompt is not in the text, and z moves from 11.31 to 11.29. Other edits measured on the same text:

- collapsing all runs of whitespace into single spaces and trimming the ends (common when text is pasted into a form): 177 tokens, $z = 10.46$;
- lowercasing everything: $z = 9.91$;
- scoring with a BERT tokenizer instead of GPT-2's: $z = -0.21$, no signal at all.

So a detector must use the generation tokenizer and normalize text the same way at generation and detection; the reference repository offers Unicode, homoglyph and truecasing normalizers for this. On 200 tokens a little drift is harmless, but on a 30-token reply every changed pair is a real share of the evidence.
